# ================= HOW YOUTUBE PICKS =================
h1('How YouTube Picks Who Sees Your Video')
para("Most people forget that YouTube by nature is a zero sum game. Say someone sits down with one hour to spare. YouTube shows them about eight videos on their homepage, and all eight are fighting for that same hour.")
para("Each time your thumbnail gets shown to someone, that's called an **impression**. Every video on that screen gets one.")
img('impressions.jpg', 6.0)
para("Now, however that person spends their hour, somebody loses. One video can take the whole hour and the other seven get nothing. Or they leave YouTube completely, and nobody wins.")
img('winner.jpg', 6.0, caption='(Eight videos, one hour. Most of them lose.)')
para("That's why \"good enough\" doesn't work on YouTube. Your video gets judged against whatever else that person could be watching instead.")
h2('YouTube Tests Your Video On Small Groups First')
para("When you upload, YouTube doesn't show your video to everyone. It shows it to a small group first, something like 100 people. If they click and watch, it gets shown to 1,000. Then 10,000, and so on up the ladder.")
img('ladder.jpg', 4.0, caption='(Credit: Amplify Views)')
para("Every step up the ladder is harder, because now you're not just competing with your niche. You're competing with everything on YouTube.")
para("Who's in that first group depends on your channel. If you're brand new, YouTube shows your video to strangers and watches what they do. If you already have an audience, it shows your subscribers first. And if your own subscribers don't click, YouTube figures strangers won't either, and it stops there.")
h2('How YouTube Knows Who To Show It To')
para("YouTube reads everything it can about your video to work out who it's for. That means your title, your thumbnail, what you actually say in the video, what's on screen, and your description.")
para("So if you sell to agency owners and you never say the words \"agency owner\", you're making YouTube guess. Say it out loud, early.")
h2("If Your Video Got 3 Views, Don't Panic")
para("On a small channel, a video with almost no views usually hasn't been tested yet. YouTube just doesn't have enough data on you, and the only way to give it data is to keep posting.")
para("Ethan Garland came to us after an eight month break from his trading channel. He was convinced his audience was dead.")
para("His second upload back did **61,000 views**. The third did **154,000**. The eighth became the most viewed video on his entire channel, at **539,000 views**, and it's still bringing him sales today.")
img('ethan_popular.jpg', 6.5, caption='(Ethan\'s channel sorted by most popular. The 539,000 and 154,000 view videos are ours.)')
para("The channel wasn't dead. It just needed videos worth pushing.")
para("**Try this:** in the first 30 seconds of your next video, say exactly who it's for, in your buyer's words. For example, \"If you run a marketing agency doing under $50,000 a month, this is for you.\"")

# ================= SATISFACTION =================
h1('What YouTube Actually Rewards: Viewers Who Are Glad They Clicked')
para("A few years ago, YouTube rewarded clicks, and everyone made clickbait. Then it rewarded watch time, and everyone made their videos longer and slower. Now the thing it cares about most is whether people were **happy they watched**.")
para("YouTube can't read minds, so it looks at what people do. Did they watch another video after yours, or close the app? Did they like it, share it or comment? Did they click \"Not interested\"? It even shows some people a quick survey after a video asking how much they liked it.")
para("That's why clickbait doesn't work anymore. A misleading thumbnail can get the click, but the person who feels tricked gives you a bad survey answer, and YouTube uses that answer to guess how people like them will feel too.")
para("You can see what a satisfied audience looks like on Inayah McMillan's channel. Our first video together did 1,185,797 views, and it has over 2,000 comments, a lot of them from people telling her how she helped them.")
h2('End On Your Best Moment')
para("People remember two parts of a video most: the best moment and the ending. Most creators end with \"thanks for watching, like and subscribe\", which means the last thing the viewer feels is bored.")
para("End on the payoff instead. Give the best result, the final answer or the punchline, and then stop.")
para("**Try this:** look at the last 30 seconds of your last video. If it's a goodbye, cut it.")

# ================= TV =================
h1('The Biggest Change For 2027: People Watch YouTube On Their TV')
para("YouTube is now the most watched app on TVs, and that changes everything about what gets pushed.")
para("When you watch YouTube on your phone and get bored, you swipe away in half a second. On a TV, you have to pick up the remote, scroll and find something else, so most people just sit back and keep watching.")
para("On one channel with over a million subscribers, the average view lasted **1 minute 27 seconds on phones and 2 minutes 48 seconds on TVs**. That's almost double. JeremyB, who studies this closely, found TV viewers' whole sessions run about 4 times longer than phone sessions.")
para("YouTube wants people to stay. So videos that work on a TV, the kind you can sit back and watch for 20 or 30 minutes, get pushed harder.")
h2('So Should You Make Longer Videos?')
para("Yes, but only if the extra minutes are worth watching. Nobody wants a 40-minute video that should have been 12.")
para("My own biggest video is a 1+ hour scriptwriting course. I filmed it in under 2 hours, and it has brought in **$275,766**.")
img('stripe.jpg', 3.5)
para("Clover Trading is another one. Before we made a single video for them, we studied 764 videos across 28 trading channels. The average video was 25.8 minutes long.")
img('clover_pair.jpg', 6.5)
para("So we went longer. Their retention at the 30-second mark went from about 20% to 65%, on videos 6 times longer than the ones they used to make. But we also learned that 40 minutes was too long for their audience. Longer works up to a point, and your audience will show you where that point is.")
para("Now, you might be thinking: \"Bryan, I can't film a 40-minute video every week.\" You don't need to. Channels that went from 10-minute videos to 14 or 18 minutes have done well. Start by adding 3 to 5 minutes of real substance, like one more example, one more client story or one more step.")
h2('The First 40 Seconds Matter More Than Ever')
para("On a TV, when someone hovers over your video, it starts playing on its own for up to 40 seconds. So the start of your video is doing the job your thumbnail used to do on its own. If those first seconds are slow, they move on.")
h2("What Else Is Winning Right Now")
para("Raw is beating polished. People trust the internet less than ever, and a lot of them are sick of content that looks fake or made by AI. A real person talking straight to the camera feels more honest.")
para("Scott Taylor's best recent video is just him drawing on an iPad. No editing at all. It got **17,700 views in under a week**, 92.7% of them from YouTube recommending it, and it booked 3 calls.")
img('scott_raw.jpg', 6.0)
para("YouTube is also pushing down mass-produced, repetitive AI videos, the kind where someone pumps out a hundred near-identical uploads. Using AI to help you write or edit is fine. Uploading videos that have no real person, story or opinion behind them is not.")
para("The two kinds of videos doing best right now are big, long, well-made videos and Shorts. The ones in the middle, mid-length and mid-effort, are getting squeezed.")
para("**Try this:** in YouTube Studio, go to Analytics, then Audience, and look at the device type. If more than 20% of your watch time comes from TVs, make your next video 3 to 5 minutes longer.")

# ================= CLICKS =================
h1("Views Don't Pay You. Clicks Do.")
para("This is the most important section in this doc, so I'm going to be really specific.")
para("The tracking we run for clients follows each sale back to the exact video that started it. Across 207 channels, 26,621 videos and over 336 million views, it has traced more than **$35 million** in sales.")
para("When we compared views to money, the connection was almost nothing. If you lined up every video by views, you couldn't predict which ones made money. Link clicks were over **4 times** better at predicting it.")
para("And the 100 most viewed videos made only about **11%** of the money. The 100 videos that made the most money had a median of about **25,000 views**, which is tiny by YouTube standards. On a normal analytics screen, some of the biggest earners look like flops.")
para("As I like to say, one view from a business owner can be worth 10,000 regular views. A random view pays you nothing. A view from someone who could buy from you is the whole game.")
h2('Some Videos Get Views. Other Videos Get Calls.')
para("Clover Trading proved this for us. Their video \"9 Years of Trading Experience in 31 Minutes\" got about 17,000 views and booked 30 calls, but only 1 of those calls turned into a sale. A strategy video with only 8,000 views made more money than both of the other videos we were testing at the time, put together.")
para("For them, psychology videos got views and strategy videos got sales. For you it might look like this: videos about the **problem** get views, and videos about **how you fix it** get calls.")
para("Scott Taylor saw the same thing. His backtest videos only got 3,000 to 4,000 views each, but the first few of them booked about 7 calls between them. Then one bigger video, \"I Finally Revealed My Most Effective Scalping Strategy (For Free)\", got 37,343 views and booked about 10 qualified calls.")
img('scott_37k.jpg', 6.0)
para("You need both kinds. Videos that bring new people in, and videos that turn them into calls.")
h2('Get More Clicks From The Views You Already Have')
para("Let's define one more number. Your **link-click rate** is the percentage of people who watched your video and then clicked a link in it. It's different from the click-through rate on your thumbnail.")
para("The median video gets almost nobody to click a link. People showed up, and the video never asked them to go anywhere.")
para("But look at what happens when you fix that. Videos where under 0.25% of viewers clicked made about **$33** each. Videos where 0.5% to 1% clicked made about **$1,395** each. Same kind of view counts, roughly 40 times the money.")
table([['Video with 4,200 views', 'Link clicks'],
       ['0.25% click', '11'],
       ['0.5% click', '21'],
       ['1% click', '42']], [3.5, 3.0])
para("You didn't get one extra view. You just changed how you asked. Doubling your views is hard and mostly out of your control. Doubling your link-click rate is a copywriting problem, and that's fully in your control.")
h2('Where To Ask For The Click')
para("Ask once, clearly, and for one thing. The best time is when the viewer is most curious, usually 60 to 70% of the way through.")
para("But if most people leave before then, ask earlier. Only about 28% of Clover's viewers made it to the end of a 30-minute video, so we added a 15-second call to action at minute 3. Link clicks went up 20% and conversions went up 25%.")
para("Put the link in the first line of your description and in a pinned comment. Send it to one place, like a booking page or a short application, and make it the logical next step from the video they just watched.")
para("**Try this:** open your last 5 videos and count how many times you asked viewers to do one specific thing. If the answer is zero, that's your biggest leak.")

# ================= TITLES =================
h1('The Titles That Get Buyers To Click Right Now')
para("The tracking data also showed which titles make money. Some of this surprised me.")
h2('How-To Titles Get Clicks But Not Customers')
para("\"How to\" titles got **32%** of all the link clicks, but only **18%** of the money. A how-to title promises to teach you something for free. The viewer learns it, feels satisfied and leaves. Nothing makes them want more.")
para("Teaching is still great. Just don't put the method in the title. Put the result in the title.")
h2('The 3 Things That Pull Buyers')
para("Titles with a **dollar amount** got 34% of clicks but 51% of the money.")
para("Titles in the **first person**, with \"I\", \"my\" or \"me\", got 37% of clicks and 52% of the money. \"I did this\" sells. \"Here's how to\" teaches.")
para("Titles with a **rate**, like \"a month\", \"a day\" or \"in 19 days\", made 4 to 5 times more money per view than average. \"$45,000\" is just a number. \"$45,000 a month\" is something a viewer can picture themselves in.")
para("Ethan Garland's two biggest videos do all three:")
img('ethan_popular.jpg', 6.5)
para("\"I Make $45,000 Per Month Trading One Simple Strategy\" has **539,000 views**. \"My Incredibly Easy Scalping Strategy To Make $2000/Day\" has **154,000**. First person, a dollar amount and a rate, in both of them.")
h2('Keep It To 10 To 12 Words')
para("Titles with 10 to 12 words made **62%** of all the money. Titles under 7 words made under 3%, even though there were over 7,000 of them.")
para("A title that makes money has to name the result, attach a number and make people curious. You can't fit all that into five words.")
h2("Don't Ask A Question")
para("Nearly 1,000 videos in the data had a question for a title. Together they made under 1% of the money. A question lets the viewer answer it in their head and move on. Make a claim instead.")
h2('Add Authority')
para("The other kind of title YouTube is pushing right now shows why people should listen to you. That might be time spent, like Clover's \"9 Years of Trading Experience in 31 Minutes\". It could also be an experiment you ran, or data you collected.")
para("Now, you might be thinking: \"Bryan, I'm a consultant. I can't put 'I made $45,000' in my title.\" That's fine. Use your clients' results, your years in the business, or the number of clients you've helped.")
h2('Rewrite Your Titles Like This')
table([['Instead of this', 'Try this'],
       ['How To Get More Consulting Clients', 'How I Booked 40 Sales Calls In 30 Days Without Paid Ads'],
       ['How To Price Your Services', 'I Raised My Price To $10,000 A Client And Booked More Calls'],
       ['Tips For Agency Owners', 'My Client Added $30,000 A Month By Changing One Boring Thing'],
       ['Is Cold Outreach Dead?', 'The Outreach Script That Books Me 3 Calls Every Single Day'],
       ['Hiring Advice For Business Owners', 'What 12 Years Of Running An Agency Taught Me About Hiring']], [2.6, 3.9])
para("Only use numbers that are true. If you put a fake number in a title, the people who believe it are the ones you'll lose first.")
h2('3 Videos That Book Calls')
bullets(['A client case study, where you walk through one client from problem to result.',
         'An objection video, which answers the main reason people don\'t buy from you, like "is this worth $10,000?"',
         'A "what I\'d do if I were you" video, where you walk a typical client through your plan.'], numbered=True)
para("**Try this:** rewrite the title of your next video with the word \"I\", a dollar amount and a time frame, in 10 to 12 words, with no question mark.")

# ================= CHANNEL SIZE =================
h1('What To Do At Your Channel Size')
h2('Under 1,000 Subscribers')
para("YouTube is showing your videos to strangers, so make videos your buyer is already searching for. Commit to 10 videos before you judge anything, because a 3-view video usually just hasn't been tested yet.")
para("Shorts are optional. They're good for getting subscribers fast, but those subscribers rarely watch your long videos. If you sell something, long-form is where people decide to buy.")
h2('1,000 To 10,000 Subscribers')
para("Your subscribers are now the first test for every video, so make things they actually want. Find the one thing holding you back, whether that's clicks, retention or topics, and fix only that.")
para("Fiction Profits Academy is a good example. They had been posting on and off for years. We gave them a steady system for ideas and scripts, and they went from 7,000 to 15,000 subscribers. They're now the top result on YouTube when someone searches \"how to start a KDP business\".")
img('fiction_search.jpg', 6.0)
para("Search keeps sending them buyers without them having to go viral.")
h2('10,000 Subscribers And Up')
para("When Scott Taylor came to us, he was convinced his channel was restricted. His videos had dropped from 14,000 to 30,000 views down to about 5,000.")
para("It wasn't restricted. His strategy had just stopped working, and nobody could tell which videos brought in money. So we tracked every booked call back to its video, and mixed videos that bring new people in with videos that turn them into calls.")
para("His views went from about 3,600 a video on our first uploads to **15,000** on average, and YouTube now brings in around **$40,000 a month**.")
para("Bigger channels don't usually need more ideas. They need to know which of their videos actually make money, and then make more of those.")

# ================= VIEW COUNT CHANGE =================
h1('YouTube Changed How It Counts Views')
para("On 27 August 2026, YouTube changed the most basic number on the platform. A view used to count only if someone stayed and watched for a few seconds. Now it counts the moment your video starts playing, even for a split second.")
para("That includes someone hovering over your thumbnail on the homepage, your video playing on its own while someone scrolls past it on their phone, and the preview that plays in search. Most of those people never chose to watch you.")
para("YouTube now calls the old kind of view an **engaged view**, meaning someone who stayed past the first few seconds. That's the number that actually matters.")
h2('How Big Is The Difference?')
para("vidIQ tracked it on their own channel the day YouTube made the switch. Their views jumped 81% from one hour to the next, without uploading anything. One new video showed over 5,000 views in its first 37 minutes, but only about 2,000 of those were engaged views. Over the next 7 days they got an extra 320,000 views and no extra money.")
para("This is one of my own videos, a 3-hour course I posted after the change:")
img('engaged_views.jpg', 3.5, caption='(58,400 views, but only 25,500 engaged views.)')
para("The public page says 58,400 views. But only **25,500** people actually stayed and watched. The other 32,900 or so were mostly thumbnails that played for a moment while someone scrolled past.")
h2('What Still Runs On Engaged Views')
para("Your click-through rate, average view duration, watch time and ad revenue are all still based on engaged views. So the extra views don't get you more money, more watch hours or more calls. YouTube doesn't play an ad to someone who scrolled past, and that person isn't going to book a call with you either.")
h2('How To See Your Real Views')
para("The easiest way is the 1of10 Chrome extension. Install it, open your video in YouTube Studio, and it shows your engaged views right under your normal view count, like in the screenshot above.")
para("If you don't want an extension, you can still find it in YouTube Studio. Go to Analytics, click Advanced mode, click the + button and add Engaged views as a column. On the YouTube Studio phone app, open a video's analytics and tap the Engagement tab.")
h2('What This Means For You')
para("Don't get excited about a bigger view count. Your new videos will look like they're beating your old ones, but anything you posted before 27 August 2026 was counted the old way. Compare engaged views to engaged views.")
para("Keep an eye on the gap. The difference between views and engaged views is mostly people who saw your video start and left straight away. If one video has a much lower share of engaged views than your usual, the start of that video probably isn't matching what the thumbnail promised.")
para("And remember what actually pays you. Nobody buys your service from a half-second preview. Judge every video on engaged views, link clicks and booked calls.")
para("**Try this:** install the 1of10 extension and write down the engaged views on your last 10 videos. That average is your real starting point, and it's the number to beat.")

# ================= TRUST =================
h1('Which Numbers To Trust (And Which To Ignore)')
para("YouTube Studio gives you a lot of numbers. Most of them mix up your fans and complete strangers, so they can fool you. Here are the three that matter, and all three are based on engaged views.")
h2('Click-Through Rate (CTR)')
para("CTR is the percentage of people who click your video after seeing the thumbnail. It drops naturally as YouTube shows your video to strangers who don't know your face. So if your views are going up and your CTR is going down, **don't touch the thumbnail**. It's working.")
para("There's also no standard CTR to aim for, because every niche is different. Compare each video to your own last 10 instead.")
h2('Retention')
para("Retention shows how many people are still watching at each second of your video.")
img('retention.jpg', 6.0)
para("Usually 30 to 60% of people leave in the first 30 seconds. That's normal, and it's why your opening matters so much. Just remember the graph is an average of everyone, so take it with a pinch of salt.")
h2('Engaged Views, Compared To Your Niche')
para("Engaged views are the best number you've got, as long as you compare them to your niche. If the average cold email video gets 1,500 views, then 10,000 is a huge win. In entertainment, 10,000 would be a flop.")
para("Go to your channel, sort your videos by most popular, and work out your average engaged views. Anything that gets 3 times your average is a win, so figure out why and make more like it.")

